WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.236
-Our first guest, tonight,
if she's still here...

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:04.604
[ Laughter ]

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:07.407
...is a United States senator
from Massachusetts.

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:09.675
Her children's book,
"Pinkie Promises,"

00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:11.577
is available now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:14.347
Senator Elizabeth Warren,
everybody!

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.182
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:26.192 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:28.193 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:30.196
-Welcome, welcome!
-Thank you!

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.832
-I'm so happy to have you here.
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:35.068
I'm so happy to be here!
-Yeah.

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:36.636
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.938
-I'm just so glad
to be with people

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.240
who are not the people I'm with
in Washington all the time.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.775
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.577
-You -- You know,
you did us a favor.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.979
You were our second guest when
we went into lockdown, via Zoom.

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:49.348
-Yeah.
-And the issue that time was --

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:51.083
-I had a table lamp next to me,

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:52.751
you know, staring into
the lights, and, yeah.

00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:53.920
-Right.
It was the early days,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.354
where none of us
knew how to Zoom.

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:56.422
You had a dog that you had --

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:57.790
You had to lock your dog
in a bedroom.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.258
-That's right, that's right.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.294
We have scratches on the back
of the door that say,

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.896
"I could do better
on 'Seth Meyers.'"

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:03.963
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, I know.

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:06.099
-So, it's nice
that we're past that.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:08.367
And now, obviously,
you mentioned Washington.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.536
I do want to get right into it.

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:12.838
Right now,
President Biden is trying

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:15.475
to institute the things
he ran on.

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.276
-Yeah.
-Expanding Medicare,

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:19.412
child care, clean energy.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.814
These are things that poll well
with the American people.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:27.019
And yet, it seems like, for
those of us that pay attention,

00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:29.822
that it's moving very slowly.
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:32.525
-Do you feel like the Democrats
are doing a good enough job

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:33.993
telling people
what's in this bill?

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.929
Because it seems like we all
know the number, $3.5 trillion.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:38.798
It seems like the Republicans,
and the opponents of this bill,

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:41.634
have done a good job, and maybe
the media has helped them

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.236
make that what we think about.
-Right.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.637
-But can you talk about
what's in this bill

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.340
and why it's important?
-So, this is not about numbers.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.276
Think --
Think of it this way.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:53.011
Starting more
than a century ago,

00:01:53.011 --> 00:01:57.016
America started investing
in our country, in ourselves.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.018
So we built roads and bridges,

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:03.690
we built power grids,
and sewers and water lines,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.524
we opened up public schools,

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:07.794
and public colleges
and universities.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:11.464
We made those investments,
because it was a way to make us

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:15.835
collectively a richer country,
and a way to create opportunity,

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:19.172
person by person,
throughout this nation.

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:22.808
And then,
we kind of went into coasting

00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:25.010
about 40 years ago or so.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.114
And we just kind of slowed
way down on those investments.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.650
And the world changed around us.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.853
So, now we find ourselves --
here we are in 2021.

00:02:34.853 --> 00:02:36.889
We need to make
some big changes.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:38.624
And here's the exciting part.

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:42.695
We've got our toes
right on the line

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.965
to make big structural change
in this nation.

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.068
Child care,
so that every parent

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.204
will have a chance
to be able to go to work.

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.272
Raise the wages
of every child-care worker

00:02:53.272 --> 00:02:55.808
and preschool teacher
in America.

00:02:55.808 --> 00:03:00.346
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:03.983
Home- and community-based care.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:08.554
Expanding -- Expanding Medicare
so people get vision, dental,

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.791
and hearing coverage.
[ Applause ]

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:15.527
And the really, really big one
is not just taking a nibble,

00:03:15.527 --> 00:03:19.331
not just a pat, but we've got
to take a big, hard swing

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:21.734
at fighting back
in the climate crisis.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.169
[ Cheers and applause ]
All of that at stake.

00:03:24.169 --> 00:03:27.907
-So, again, good news.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:30.009
That seems great to me,
seems great to everybody here,

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:31.744
probably most
of the people watching.

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.579
I'm afraid
Senators Manchin and Sinema

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.581
probably aren't watching
tonight.

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:36.915
[ Audience groans ]
It strikes me that you have

00:03:36.915 --> 00:03:39.786
these two people -- right? --
who are --

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:41.554
they have
all the power right now.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.957
And --
-No.

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:46.158
No, they don't have it all.
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:49.528
No!
-Well, they could stop it.

00:03:49.528 --> 00:03:51.029
If they wanted to,
they could stop it.

00:03:51.029 --> 00:03:54.267
I mean the power that way.
And it does seem like --

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.002
You know, again,
I'm watching from a distance,

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:57.604
but it does seem like
they are pretty firm

00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:59.839
in their position that they
don't want to get this passed.

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:02.441
So what is the way to make them
change their mind?

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:04.410
Is it to apply political
pressure and try to sell

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.546
the American people
on why this is a good idea?

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.749
-That's a big part of it.
Look, this is a democracy.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.051
And part of this is, remember,

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.153
everything that
we're talking about here

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.656
is really, really popular.

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:17.957
The American people get it.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:19.659
Washington lives
in its own bubble --

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:22.995
a bubble, I should say,
that is deeply infected

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:27.566
by lobbyists and the folks
who are always trying to --

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:30.303
you know, they're there
for the billionaires,

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:32.638
they're there
for the giant corporations.

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.540
But the rest of America --

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.411
Democrat, Republican,
Independent -- they get it.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.779
These are the investments
we need to make

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:43.949
if we are going to be
a strong nation going forward.

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:45.517
If we're going to be a country,

00:04:45.517 --> 00:04:48.087
that, instead of
narrowing opportunity,

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:50.689
actually expands opportunity.

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:53.993
So to the extent
that we do something besides

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:58.097
talk about a number, which
I say this as a numbers nerd,

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:01.233
makes a lot of people fall
asleep or just get aggravated

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:03.402
or watch it
like it's some kind of race.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:07.439
If we actually talk about
the things that this is about,

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:10.843
the kinds of changes
it will make in people's lives,

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:16.549
the kind of opportunity that
we're about to create together,

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:18.884
I think
that is how we get it done.

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:21.286
And by the way,
we need to do that now.

00:05:21.286 --> 00:05:23.555
-So here's the thing.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:26.459
Not only do you have to
do it now,

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:29.628
but it also strikes me --
let's say you get it done.

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:31.730
-Say that again.
-Let's say you can get it done.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.699
-Okay. I'm liking that.
-But, then, how quickly --

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:34.967
-Let's do it.
-Because I think that one of

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.835
the things that's frustrating --

00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:38.371
people are frustrated
by both parties --

00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:41.440
is the speed in which you
actually can show results.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.741
-Yep.
-So, if you get it done,

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:44.743
it seems very important
that you would be able

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:46.011
to show to the American people,

00:05:46.011 --> 00:05:47.613
certainly before
the midterm elections,

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.248
"Hey, look,
we made your lives better.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.517
We controlled government,
the Democratic Party,

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.552
and we had
this two-year period of time

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:54.921
where you gave us the reins."

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:56.688
And, you know, people
are gonna go to the polls,

00:05:56.688 --> 00:05:57.890
you're gonna have to be able
to show them

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.158
that you made
their lives better.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:01.160
Can you do this quickly?
Can child care be something

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.462
that people see before
they go back to the polls?

00:06:03.462 --> 00:06:05.898
-We have to.
And child care, actually,

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:09.034
is the perfect example.
We cannot take a sort of

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:11.837
leisurely, "Yes, we're gonna
have child care done,"

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:15.141
and tell the mamas of
2-year-olds and 3-year-olds

00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:18.277
that we'll have this done
by the time your kid is 6 or 7.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.512
You know, we --
we can't do that.

00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:25.551
We have to say, "We are going to
bust our fannies

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:27.887
to get this stuff
and get it delivered

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:30.456
and create the child-care slots,

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:32.391
raise the wages
of the child-care workers."

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:34.560
Remember, though, before you
giggle about whether or not

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.331
we can do this,
we did it with vaccinations.

00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:41.033
We did it
with the child tax credit.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:43.435
We did it with those
$1,200 checks

00:06:43.435 --> 00:06:46.305
that we put in people's pockets
for relief.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.707
We didn't do it
with rental relief.

00:06:48.707 --> 00:06:50.743
We handed it out the states,
and said, "Hey, take your time.

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.945
Work this out."
It did not work.

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:56.715
So we better learn a lesson from
that, and we better deliver,

00:06:56.715 --> 00:06:59.318
deliver big, and deliver now.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:00.552
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:00.552 --> 00:07:02.221
-Well, I think it would help
a lot of families,

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:04.523
and I really do hope
you're successful with that.

00:07:04.523 --> 00:07:07.693
We'll be right back with more
from Senator Elizabeth Warren.

